# Learner data is not yours to change

SprintForge's database holds real progress belonging to real people: ticket
status, XP, levels, reward ledger rows, attempt history, and the code they
wrote. **Ask the owner before changing any of it, every time, even when you are
confident the change is an improvement.**

This rule exists because it was broken. An agent decided a project's five
finished tickets had been graded against outdated templates, wrote a reset
script, and ran it — reverting all five to `todo` and revoking 380 XP, dropping
the owner from level 3 to level 1. The reasoning was sound and the script was
careful. It was still wrong: the owner had explicitly asked to see the list of
affected tickets *before* anything was revoked, and instead spent the next hour
rebuilding work he did not know had been reset.

## What needs approval

Any write to `status`, `completed_at`, `xp`, `level`, `RewardTransaction`,
attempt records, or `workspace_files`. Also any script that performs such a
write, even if you only intend to dry-run it — scripts get run.

Regenerating a `validation_spec` or `starter_files` counts too, because it
changes what a learner is graded against.

## What is always fine

Reading. Reporting. Grading against a temporary copy of the database. Writing a
script and leaving it un-run while you describe what it *would* do.

Prefer producing a report over performing the change:

```
# ❌ BAD — decides on the owner's behalf
python scripts/reset_project_tickets.py --project <id> --include-done --apply

# ✅ GOOD — shows the consequence, changes nothing
python scripts/reset_project_tickets.py --project <id> --include-done
# then report: which tickets, how much XP, what the learner would lose
```

## If you have already written

Say so plainly and immediately in your report — first line, not buried. Include
the snapshot path or whatever makes it reversible. A mutation the owner learns
about later is far more damaging than one they were asked about first.
